==Problem==
Rectangle <imath>ABCD</imath> has sides <imath>CD=3</imath> and <imath>DA=5</imath>. A circle of radius <imath>1</imath> is centered at <imath>A</imath>, a circle of radius <imath>2</imath> is centered at <imath>B</imath>, and a circle of radius <imath>3</imath> is centered at <imath>C</imath>. Which of the following is closest to the area of the region inside the rectangle but outside all three circles?

<asy>
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--(0,0));
draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw(Circle((0,3),2));
draw(Circle((5,3),3));
label("A",(0.2,0),W);
label("B",(0.2,2.8),NW);
label("C",(4.8,2.8),NE);
label("D",(5,0),SE);
label("5",(2.5,0),N);
label("3",(5,1.5),E);
</asy>

<imath> \text{(A) }3.5\qquad\text{(B) }4.0\qquad\text{(C) }4.5\qquad\text{(D) }5.0\qquad\text{(E) }5.5 </imath>
==Solution 1==
The area in the rectangle but outside the circles is the area of the rectangle minus the area of all three of the quarter circles in the rectangle.

The area of the rectangle is <imath>3\cdot5 =15</imath>. The area of all 3 quarter circles is <imath>\frac{\pi}{4}+\frac{\pi(2)^2}{4}+\frac{\pi(3)^2}{4} = \frac{14\pi}{4} = \frac{7\pi}{2}</imath>. Therefore the area in the rectangle but outside the circles is <imath>15-\frac{7\pi}{2}</imath>.  <imath>\pi</imath> is approximately <imath>\dfrac{22}{7},</imath> and substituting that in will give <imath>15-11=\boxed{\text{(B) }4.0}</imath>

==Solution 1.5 (People who don't know how to do the area of a sector)==
Instead of calculating the area of the rectangle minus the area of the 3 quarter-circles that are in it, we calculate the 

area of the 3 circles, not just the quarter-circles. <imath>3^2\pi</imath> is equal to <imath>9\pi</imath>, <imath>2^2\pi</imath> squared is equal to <imath>4\pi</imath>, and 

<imath>1^2\pi</imath> is equal to 1. It is obvious that they are all quarter-circles, as mentioned in solution 1, so you divide each of 

them by <imath>4</imath>. So they become <imath>\pi</imath>, <imath>\frac{9}{4}\pi</imath>, and <imath>\frac{1}{4}\pi</imath>. Adding them together, it's <imath>\frac{14}{4}\pi</imath>. 

Now you can calculate <imath>15-\frac{14}{4}\pi</imath>, and you'll get around <imath>4.00000000000001</imath>, which is closest to <imath>\boxed{\text{(B) }4.0}</imath>

